  "answer": "The supplied passages give a qualified answer, rather than a general rule that the target market determines offensiveness.\n\n**Manual practice:** Some trade marks are regarded as scandalous regardless of the market, particularly those promoting racial vilification, religious intolerance or personal abuse of an individual (TMM/Part30/2/2/11).\n\nContext can nevertheless matter. For Indigenous material of significant cultural, spiritual or religious significance, the Manual directs consideration of the proposed goods and services, sensitivity to Indigenous people, ownership and other mitigating or aggravating factors. Evidence that the mark or its proposed use would not offend the relevant Indigenous community may support reconsideration of a ground for rejection (TMM/Part30/2/2/13). This supports considering context in that setting, but does not establish a general target-market test.\n\nThe Manual also requires the scandalous element to be obvious and up front: a mere suggestion or vague possibility of offence is insufficient (TMM/Part30/2/2/4).\n\n**Legislation:** Section 42(a) itself is not supplied. The supplied Act passages do not answer the question about scandalous marks, so the conclusions above describe Manual practice, not the statutory wording."
